hi this is presh talwalkar we'll start  out the proof by noticing that two  squared is equal to two plus two  or we're adding two to itself for a  total of two times  this pattern is true for other numbers  when we have 3 squared  that's the same as adding 3 to itself  for a total of 3 times  similarly  4 squared  is equal to four  added to itself for a total of four  times  in general  x squared is equal to x added to itself  for a total of x  times this is the equation that will be  the basis for our paradox  we'll start out with x squared is equal  to x added to itself for a total of x  times  now we're going to use some calculus  we're going to take the derivative  of each side  the derivative of x squared is equal to  2x on the right hand side the derivative  of x is equal to one  the derivative of x again is equal to  one  and then we keep adding this so each  time we add a one to the derivative of x  and we do this for a total of x times  if we add 1 to itself a total of x times  we find that 2x is equal to x  and this is a problem because this has  to be true for every value of x so it  doesn't matter if we put x equals one x  equals a half  we end up with the result that two is  equal to one  and this is strange because it seems  like every step we've done has been  valid but we've come to a contradictory  result  so can you figure it out  i bet if you pose this to your math  teacher  many times your math teacher won't know  exactly where the proof went wrong  but i'm going to show it to you so that  you can explain to your teacher why this  proof isn't correct  the beginning equation is correct x  squared is equal to x plus itself a  total of x times  when we take the derivative of x squared  we get 2x that's correct but the mistake  comes at this step when we take the  derivative of x added to itself x times  we cannot simply add each term one by  one by taking the derivative  and there's a reason for this when we  add x to itself a total of x times this  is not a simple linear equation where we  take the derivative of each term  we really have x times itself  so in order to compute the derivative of  x plus x a total of x times we really  have a sum that's involving x  so in order to take this derivative we  need to use the product rule  so we take  the first term times the derivative of  the second so we have x  times 1 plus the second term of x times  the derivative of the first term x to  get 1.  when we do it this way we find that 2x  is equal to 2x and of course this is  true  and so there's no paradox at all here  there's no contradiction  so the problem in the proof is you can't  simply take the derivative of each term  because it's not a simple linear  expression it's an expression of x that  involves a total of x times  i hope you like this video please  subscribe to my channel i make videos on  math and game theory you can catch me on  my blog mind your decisions  you can also follow that on facebook  patreon and google plus  you can also catch me at preshtalwalker  on twitter instagram tumblr and  pinterest  and please check out my books i've  written some pretty cool books about  math i think you'll enjoy them there's  some mental math tricks and math puzzles  and there's a bunch of game theory as  well  so check it out there's a link in the  video description and thanks for  watching
 

